# tool: Jobs

What the daemon has deferred to run later, and whether that work is healthy.

While it serves, the daemon drains this queue and makes a periodic pass on the
scheduler's tick (fifteen minutes unless the installation sets another): it
hands back work whose worker stopped reporting, queues the scheduled routines
that are due in every workspace it serves, and removes finished jobs older than
a week. A process that only runs one command does none of that.

Only work handed to the queue is here. A scheduled routine's run is a job on
the routine queue: tried once, failed with the run's own reason, and not queued
again while an earlier one for the same routine is still waiting or running.
Conversation turns, task runs, and routines fired by hand, by a webhook or by an
activity start directly and never become jobs, so an empty queue is the
ordinary state: to learn whether one of those happened, read its conversation (a
failed turn is recorded on the message that asked for it), the routine's runs,
or the activity log.

## Commands
- **list** — the jobs, filtered by queue, status, workspace or kind
- **get** — one job with its payload, its result and its error
- **stats** — the shape of the queue: how many in each state, what is stuck
- **recover** — hand back jobs whose worker stopped reporting
- **purge** — remove finished jobs older than the window

## When to use
- **When deferred work did not happen:** stats first, then list the dead ones
- **After a crash:** recover, to return the work its worker was holding

## When NOT to use
- Not to find out whether a turn, a task run or a routine fired by hand
  happened; those are not jobs, and every routine run, scheduled or not, is in
  the routine's runs
- Not to schedule work — work is enqueued by whatever owns it
- Not as a log of what an agent did; that is the activity log

Composite tool `Jobs` with 5 actions: get, list, purge, recover, stats.

There is no enqueue here, deliberately. A queue anything can write to is a queue
whose contents nobody can attribute.

A job marked claimed whose lease has lapsed is not a busy job: it is one whose
worker died. Stats separates the two, and recover is what fixes it.

A dead job is kept rather than deleted. "It ran and failed three times" and "it
never arrived" are different problems.

## Usage
Call as `Jobs({ action: "<action>", input: { ... }, _reasoning: "..." })`.
Set `schema: true` on the same level as `action` to receive the full action detail (description, examples, input schema, token estimate) instead of executing.

## actions

### jobs_get

Read one job.

One job in full: what it was asked to do, how many times it has been tried, what it produced, and what went wrong.

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "id": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Identifier of the job."
    },
    "_reasoning": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "MANDATORY. NEVER FORGET. Explain why this specific tool is being called now, what outcome you expect, and the immediate next step if that helps clarify the call. Do not leave this empty.",
      "minLength": 1
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "id",
    "_reasoning"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}
```

### jobs_list

List the queued work.

The jobs the daemon holds, newest first, filtered by queue, status, workspace or handler kind.

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "queue": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "One of: chat, task, routine, workspace."
    },
    "status": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "One of: pending, claimed, succeeded, failed, dead.",
      "enum": [
        "pending",
        "claimed",
        "succeeded",
        "failed",
        "dead"
      ]
    },
    "workspace": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Only jobs of this workspace."
    },
    "kind": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Only jobs of this handler kind."
    },
    "limit": {
      "type": "integer",
      "description": "How many to return, newest first."
    },
    "_reasoning": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "MANDATORY. NEVER FORGET. Explain why this specific tool is being called now, what outcome you expect, and the immediate next step if that helps clarify the call. Do not leave this empty.",
      "minLength": 1
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "_reasoning"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}
```

### jobs_purge

Remove finished jobs older than the window.

Drop succeeded and dead jobs past the retention window, seven days by
default.

Only terminal jobs go. Anything pending, claimed or awaiting a retry stays
whichever window you ask for.

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "olderThanDays": {
      "type": "integer",
      "description": "Override the window, in days. Defaults to 7."
    },
    "_reasoning": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "MANDATORY. NEVER FORGET. Explain why this specific tool is being called now, what outcome you expect, and the immediate next step if that helps clarify the call. Do not leave this empty.",
      "minLength": 1
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "_reasoning"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}
```

### jobs_recover

Hand back work whose worker stopped reporting.

Return every job whose lease has lapsed to the queue.

A worker renews its lease while it runs. One that is killed stops, the lease
expires, and this is what makes the work it was holding runnable again rather
than a permanent hole in the queue.

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "_reasoning": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "MANDATORY. NEVER FORGET. Explain why this specific tool is being called now, what outcome you expect, and the immediate next step if that helps clarify the call. Do not leave this empty.",
      "minLength": 1
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "_reasoning"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}
```

### jobs_stats

The shape of the queue right now.

How many jobs in each state and on each queue, with the two lists that
matter: what died, and what is held by a worker that stopped reporting.

The second is the shape of a real incident. A claimed job whose lease has lapsed
is not busy — its worker is gone, and recover is what returns it.

```json
{
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "workspace": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "Only jobs of this workspace."
    },
    "_reasoning": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "MANDATORY. NEVER FORGET. Explain why this specific tool is being called now, what outcome you expect, and the immediate next step if that helps clarify the call. Do not leave this empty.",
      "minLength": 1
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "_reasoning"
  ],
  "additionalProperties": false
}
```

